Lince, the philosophy (@philosophy: 1, is #chapter, #instinct: 1, #done) {
It is possible to find many different philosophies to divide life. The Lince tool separates it into Needs and Contributions to Needs. The Lince philosophy is to meet your Needs and Contribute to the Needs of those you care about; using the Lince tool or not. The maximum point of the Lince philosophy is all Needs deemed fair being met, and in the void of unmet Needs... We don't know yet.
}

Lince, the tool (@tool: 1, is #chapter, #instinct: 2, #done) { r_YJQ36ETAKE8J7F8Y65DG7V43MB
> **Everything is a Record. Every change is a Fact. Every intended change is a Promise.**

Lince models everything with a Record, it has title and description as text columns and a quantity, the quantity is thought to be the most changed field, representing state that can be affected by math in automations and Transfers. Lince thinks by default that when a quantity of a record is negative, it means an Actor Needs that much of the Record in question, so '-1 Apple' is the Need of 1 apple, if it is positive it's a possible Contribution, to oneself or others, if it's zero, its neither a resource we can use nor a Need.

Records can have a long title, and can also be given a slug (nickname), like @my-record for easy referencing. We can also give #tags to records, called Assertions. We can tag something as '#done', or link it to another like '#needs @my-other-record'. These can be anything you want #needs-testing, #food, #v1 #part-of @my-record...

These Assertions are done by you, and they mean something to you. Other people may have different meanings, so when you apply an Assertion, you can say it is the #apple from a Vocabulary, coming from someone's Lince. So when both users use the same #apple from the same Vocabulary, they mean the same thing. This is useful for setting equal units so people can trade things.

When something changes, we save a Fact, saying the change to a Record was to gain +1, made by this part of Lince or this person. We have work metadata like assignees, dates, work log for hours, etc.

A Lince is called a Cell. Each Cell can be part of many groups, that may represent such group called an Organ. The Organ can be the group of your devices like your phone and computer that represent you. Or it can be a group for your work, education, friends, family, hobbies, communities. Your devices/Cells will be part of many Organs, making a very connected network. Each Cell can have a lot of users inside, that log in to change the data there or sync with their Cells. Each Lince can also sync data with the files on computer in many formats, having a constant file backup of your data in Lince, being able to use it in different apps.

You can make Transfers with other people/nodes, by agreeing to levels of resources both parties will get in return, or not, for donations. They are all promises, and they unlock all types of economic activites and several types of organization. You can use for donations, organize buying lists for parties saying people will bring certain items. One person may receive a raw material and promise to give to next person a final product, modeling transparently for all the network a public supply chain, or make it private. Or you can buy products and services like delivery apps and ride sharing, or use as a big purchase platform.

You can create automations and recurrent tasks in lince with Karma rules, there will be a value as the condition, that pulls information from your data if you want to, if it is a certain value it passes to bring a consequence. This and other points, to receive and create computer terminal commands and interact with other systems. Mechanisms to learn from your data, and make suggestions and even automations and transfers with no finger lifted. All locally, all activated with consent on the user, no data leaking to servers.

With this philosophy we can automate the remembering part of habits, creating a need for them everyday, we can assign work tasks, buy, borrow and donate items, create roadmaps for others to follow, in the areas of production and education, and do much more.

# Interfaceless — The Death of Lince

Most software is repetitive Create, Read, Update, Delete (CRUD) operations. We can create a good general base for basic CRUD software, and continue to expand beyond that. The diversity in what can be created with it is the real power.

People will have access to a completely free and open source todo app, event planner, financial analysis platform, industrial production planning and more; all with a change of data (DNA) and maybe some extensions.

Once the operational part of life is modeled, automated and interconnected, the effects of having built a robust and generic base for interaction with systems will start to show. If there is no incentive to rebuild a management system/platform (because there is a clearly G.O.A.T.ed one), more efforts will have to be focused on new highly specific innovative and optimizing ideas. People will enjoy having previously centralized, private and paywalled capabilities now in close reach for free.

Think of platforms for purchasing items or car rides, they are companies that get a cut, to orchestrate something that computationally a global peer-to-peer network personal devices could easily manage. Think of how much your much time your devices spend processing your online purchases and how much it could be plugged into the LNHM (Lince-Net-Hive-Mind).

Item purchase platforms in Lince are for Records of item Contributions, your purchases are Transfers. The car rides you request on are Transfers of money for 'Transportation from point A to B'. Any automation they make inside their platforms is limited to the data they have on you, which is a miniscule amount of your whole life (at least I hope it is). If you where to set up hacky ways to use those platforms automatically to fit your needs, you would be doing it to every app you have, connecting them in ways they where not meant to, possibly programming something to organize all those changing external apps. It doesn't need to be that inefficient, you can have one central program that you can trust to work offline for you, acting just the way you want, featureful, built by many people, for it is the main one.

The model of Lince can be a superset of many apps, and can spark many different markets and support people with it's usage. Those that occupy their time building the basic operations of life now with a good generic base can focus fully on the differential part of their Contributions to the world: the steps, the insights, the algorithms, a higher quality.

This is just an abstraction: When you install Lince (the tool) you have a Lince Cell, with it's personal data/behavior (DNA). When a cell is used by several people we call that an Organ. When you connect with other Lince nodes, you assume they are possibly Organs, you dont know how they function internally. Lince, the philosophy, is when all Cells and Organs work together to satiate everyone's Needs.

The assumption is that an app that is useful for a lot of cases and incentivizes people to help each other, with no extra friction, is the best use of technology we can have. It will not solve anything by itself, but is is the best tool in the software part of the world.

Like every good Contribution, the goal is to not have to Contribute anymore; because it was a one time Need, or the Frequent Need has been solved.

Lince is a Contribution to the Need of having Lince, of having all production possibly connected, so there is predictability in resource usage and logistics coordination.

It's a Contribution to the disorganized construction of solutions glued by the internet, centralized in platforms that worsen by the day.

It's a Contribution to understanding the whole of possibilities of how you can connect with other people, after seeing Needs you didnt know others had, sparking ideas of Contributions you didn't know you could make.

The death of Lince is the death of the Need of Lince. If there is ever any configuration of resources that makes Lince useless, then it's job is fullfilled, there is nothing else to be done, no commit, no push, just vibes. Untill then, a system for self organization that can turn into a dance of the world is, to some, something useful and exciting to build.

How can we kill Lince. Lince is the need for Lince. How can we deliver Lince so that we dont need to do what Lince does. Lince can help us with transactions and automations, with communication and knowledge gathering. With the Need of Lince over we can use it for such great and fun things.

Is our best case scenario to have a hardware that is wearable in the head, something everybody has, like a crown? A Lince Crown? With an hability to scan your surroundings for Needs and Contributions of Lince: a Bed (that Need's to be made), a Need of Apple close to you, that you can Contribute because the big pile of Apples in front of you will not be all consumed in time and will rot.

We want to minimize the time spent managing the meeting of Needs and Contributions. The rest is just doing things, living life. We need a major version of Lince that will kill it. It might be a v2.0.0, but it Needs a lot of thinking: a realistic best case scenario, and the Contributions for it to kill Lince.

A team is being assembled, to fight the battle of killing Lince. Will you join the party?

| "Death is only the beginning" - The Mummy.
} r_YJQ36ETAKE8J7F8Y65DG7V43MB


Installation (@installation: 0, #instinct, #part-of @tool) {

Run a downloaded binary with `./lince`, or `lince` if it is on your path. From the repository, run `cargo run -p lince`. The normal desktop run needs no `.env` file or environment variables. Lince keeps its data in your user config directory by default. Use `--directory /path/to/data` to choose another place.

For an isolated development Cell, run `mise dev`. It uses `test_lince` in your user config directory and port 6176. Set `LINCE_DATA_DIR` and `LINCE_PORT` for one run, or pass `--directory` and `--port` directly:
```bash
LINCE_DATA_DIR=/tmp/my-lince LINCE_PORT=6200 mise dev
mise run dev -- --directory /tmp/my-lince --port 6200
```
When Nix is installed, `mise dev` enters the interface development shell on NixOS, other Linux systems, and macOS. Otherwise it uses the local Cargo toolchain. No `.env` file is needed.

A headless server needs an admin account on first start. Give it a password file that only the service user can read. The NixOS module is imported as `inputs.lince.nixosModules.default`; set `services.lince.enable = true` and choose `services.lince.initialAdminPasswordFile`. The institute host at `institute/configuration.nix` chooses `/var/lib/lince/initial-admin-password` and listens on `127.0.0.1:6174`. Put the password file on that host before expecting the service to start. The host file assumes UEFI boot, an ext4 root filesystem labeled `nixos`, and a FAT boot filesystem labeled `ESP`. The deploy workflow checks those mounts before switching.

For the browser Facade, build with the `facade` feature, then give the public origin and local listen address. The origin can also be set with `LINCE_FACADE_ORIGIN`:
```bash
cargo run -p lince --no-default-features --features facade -- --server --facade \
  --facade-origin https://lince.mycompany.mydomain \
  --listen-addr 127.0.0.1:6174 \
  --initial-admin-password-file /path/to/initial-admin-password
```


### 3. Docker

The published container image is public at `ghcr.io/lince-social/lince`. The container keeps its state under `/var/lib/lince` and is meant to be run with a persistent volume.

```bash
# For new Ubuntu VPS you can first run
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y docker.io
sudo systemctl enable --now docker

# To keep Lince running as a background service and bring it back after reboot, run it with `-d` and `--restart unless-stopped`:
docker run -d \
  --name lince \
  --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 6174:6174 \
  -v lince-data:/var/lib/lince \
  ghcr.io/lince-social/lince:rolling
```

The location of Lince's configuration directory depends on your operating system:

| Platform | Value                                 | Example                                        |
| -------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Linux    | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` or `$HOME`/.config | /home/alice/.config/lince                      |
| macOS    | `$HOME`/Library/Application Support   | /Users/Alice/Library/Application Support/lince |
| Windows  | `{FOLDERID_RoamingAppData}`           | C:\Users\Alice\AppData\Roaming\lince           |

The directory and lince.toml is created automatically on first start and gives you a random auth secret. To enable auth you will need to add an `enable = true`, example:

```bash
[auth]
secret = "75b4c1a538af41a79b19ac17cbe03829"
enabled = true
```

When auth is enabled, Lince will require an admin user to exist. If none exists yet, run Lince once in a terminal so it can prompt you to create the initial admin user.

Accessing it from the outside world:

```bash
# If your VPS uses `ufw`, open the HTTP port:
sudo ufw allow 6174/tcp

# If you have a domain, you can use Caddy to setup a reverse proxy and make requests with HTTPS
sudo apt install -y caddy
sudo mkdir -p /etc/caddy
cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
foo.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:6174
}
EOF
sudo ufw allow 80
sudo ufw allow 443
sudo systemctl enable --now caddy
```


}

Record (@record: 1, #instinct, #part-of @tool) {
	
### Every Need is one Record

Lince starts from a claim that sounds too simple to be useful: everything you might want software to help you with is a **Need**. And a Need always shows up in one of three shapes.

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Need"] --> B["Action: Habit, Task"]
  A --> C["Item: Food, Tool"]
  A --> D["Goal: Milestone, Dream"]
```

Take one apple and all three appear at once:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Need"] --> B["Action: eat an apple"]
  A --> C["Item: an apple"]
  A --> D["Goal: be someone who plants apple trees"]
```

_A habit, a thing, and a life direction — the same subject at three scales._

Most software picks one shape and builds an app around it: a to-do app for actions, an inventory app for items, a goal tracker for goals. Then your apples live in three places that cannot talk to each other. Lince refuses the split. Every Need becomes one Record.

### The other half: contribution

A Need on its own does nothing. Something has to meet it — and whatever meets it is a **Contribution**. Between the two sits the thing being moved.

```mermaid
graph RL
  A["-1 Need"] --> B["Apple"]
  B --> C["1 Contribution"]
```

_Before: someone is short one apple, someone else has one spare._

Watch the numbers. A Need is a **negative** quantity — something missing. A Contribution is a **positive** one — something available. Meeting the Need is the two moving toward each other until both sides are zero: nothing missing and nothing spare.

So Need and Contribution are not two concepts at all. They are one concept with opposite signs, and instead of an app about apples you get a Record that everything about apples hangs off:

```mermaid
graph RL
  A["Record: apples"]
  B["Need: eat an apple"] --> A
  C["Need: an apple"] --> A
  D["Need: plant apple trees"] --> A
  E["Contribution: an apple"] --> A
```

### What a Record actually holds

The **title** and **description** are for you — they carry the human meaning. The **quantity** is for you or the machine: it is the state, and it is the part rules read and change.

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Record"] --> B["Quantity: -1"]
  A --> C["Head: Apple"]
  A --> D["Body: the description"]
  A --> E["...and more"]
```

_"I am one apple short." That is a complete, working Record._

A number is deliberately unopinionated. The same field can mean stock, debt, need, budget, progress, votes, hours, or responsibility — which is what lets one kind of Record carry every workflow you have, instead of a new table per feature. Every change to a quantity is recorded as a **Fact**: who changed it, when, and why. Nothing moves invisibly, and turning on `facts` in a Protein shows a sand the whole trail.

A Record as mentioned so far is plain, basic. There are workflows that are not just modeling ideas, they need extra features. The 'kind' property is a way to specialize the Record, into being many possible things. It can be a rule, signal, transfer, decision, device, organ, person, protein, sand, conversation, thread, message, thread-invite, program, frequency, grant, and call-session. If the built-in features of Lince are not enough, one can use a record.extension to save data, and build custom logic in the sands to use that data, this is like an everything-goes field to store what you want.

A record's quantity can name its **unit** — kilograms, hours, a currency — by pointing at a Concept, so "3" is never ambiguous. A Record can name a **place**, so it shows up on a map or in whatever is near you.
- [ ] Maybe a Record can have longitute,latitude, and be given a name, so we can use it as an address, and in the links of a record of party we put like location or address link so Party #address Street4 (that has a longitude) or just plainly in the Party record, since it will also have latitude longitude. It's good to be able to use both.

It remembers which **Organ** it came from, which is how you know whether something arrived from a group you are in or you wrote it yourself. And it keeps **when it was created and last changed**.
}


Assertion (@assertion: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {
Records can be given ideas they represent:

Subject --> Predicate
Apple       #apple

That is called applying a concept to a Record.

When you make an assertion that a Record is something in relation to another Record, you are making a link:

Subject --> Predicate --> Object
Hand        #part-of      Arm

An assertion can have the place of the Identity of a Record, being the concept that represents that apple most. When you want to understand what something is that has a bunch of assertions, you look for the main one, the Identity:

Apple is #apple

}



Organ (@organ: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology, #done) { r_VAX3HA21RXF9AENHMCJTSEF1AT
Every Record so far lived alone on one machine. This is about what happens when
people show up: your own Cell, other people’s Cells, and the Organs that let
several Cells hold something in common.

## The Cell

Everything so far happened in one place: your **Cell**. A Cell is your own Lince — your records, on your machine, answering to you.

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Cell"]
```

## Cells Have No Main One

Other people have their own. No Cell is inside any other, and none of them is the "main" one:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Cell"]
  B["Cell"]
  C["Cell"]
  D["Cell"]
```

## The Organ

When several Cells want to hold something in common — a household budget, a team backlog, a shared shopping list — they form an **Organ**.

```mermaid
graph TD
  O["Organ"]
  A["Cell"] --> O
  B["Cell"] --> O
  C["Cell"] --> O
  D["Cell"] --> O
```

_An Organ is a shared context, not a server that owns the members._

## Organs Do Not Blur Together

You belong to as many Organs as you have contexts, and they do not blur into each other. Work does not see your hobby; your family does not see your work backlog.

```mermaid
graph TD
  B["Work"] --> A["Your Cell"]
  C["Hobby"] --> A
  D["Family"] --> A
  E["Friends"] --> A
```

## Lince Has No Centre

Lince as a whole is just all of it — Cells and Organs, with no centre:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Organ"] --> O["Lince"]
  B["Cell"] --> O
  C["Organ"] --> O
  D["Cell"] --> O
```
This is why the frontend keeps **areas** (in the workspace menu) separate from each other, and why a Protein can filter by organ. The boundary is real, and it is the same boundary the next chapter has to cross.

Other Organs can be saved as contacts, with unknown, known and blocked states, and a numeric Proximity property that you can use to indicate the value of your bond with them. In Transfers, you may use it to offer to people you have only a certain level of Proximity with, perhaps increasing the range of which you may donate something over time by increasing needed proximity to see such donation if closer contacts dont immediatelly want it.

} r_VAX3HA21RXF9AENHMCJTSEF1AT


Lingua (@lingua: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {

For File Sync, Lingua is the syntax of the files on disk, that gets a bidirectional sync with the database.

## Vocabulary

We call a Vocabulary the bundle of Assertions that come from an Organ, as if we create some dialect, some units, and they mean something to us. When people use the same Vocabulary it is expected that they are speaking about the same thing. So if a Lingua file is for talking to the file system with sync, a Lingua Vocabulary is for talking with other people with common ground. Like a dictionary, with the meanings you give it and Records as examples with text, photo, video, links, all that Records have to make the Vocabulary an encyclopedia of knowledge, each thing that is an identity can be consulted to understand from the Vocabulary this identity belongs to what is it's definition, it's meaning. We can have an Organ deemed by us as the official when talking about a subject, we just such and such Vocabulary. Like a standard, ISO.
}


Areas of Influence (@areas-of-influence: 0, #instinct, #part-of @interface) {

Areas of Influence are shapes you put in the canvas. They can have some logic behind them:

- Protein Spawn: your data can be pulled to the interface, with the schema you chose based on filters and aggregation of data manifested into some sand/castle.
- Attraction/Repulsion: they can attract sands or repel them. Based on a property from db they hold, or just affect general sands.
- Changing Properties: when Sands/Castles enter or leave the area, they can gain or lose some property in the records that are represented in the sand (through Protein).

And many more. They are one way to add logic and customization to your experience on the interface.
}

Blood (@blood: 0, #instinct, #part-of @ontology) {
If your Lince is a Cell, we call Blood the capability of interacting with other systems. Several protocols exist that allow for really useful apps, we should try to integrate with that: Nostr, irc, Matrix, ActivityPub... And not only protocols but simple file formats, the act of saving something to a simple markdown already allows for so much. Putting the mapped Karma into .ics may be a very simple way of scheduling changes to Records.

This is a good step in interoperability of systems, less isolation and complex APIs and more common formats easy to deal with, allowing for extraction of data in a simple, readable and continuous way.
}

Karma (@karma: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #done) { r_K7T3ZG08G5EBWRSTF8NBWMTZYV
Karma means 'action' in Sanskrit. In Lince it is the sum of all automations and intelligence. Without Karma in Lince, all the data is like still water if not touched by the user. Karma is the scheduler, the automatic action taker, it changes quantites, fires computer commands, receives requests from other systems and saves the result to be used in the Rules. All of it is optional, it can be set to take every Fact as an evidence that a certain Karma Rule should happen in such and such way. A pattern can be understood and have a growth function, so that after 3 months of buying apples every week you create an automation around posting a need for apples and a transfer proposal for such, all with hands-on control or complete automation. 

Rules are meant to be math equations that pull valus from your data and other systems to compute Conditions. These Conditions are checked to see if they pass a Threshold, if they do they enact a Consequence.

Everything up to here you do by hand. **Karma** is the part of Lince that does things without you — it watches Records, does arithmetic on them, and changes them, on a beat or when something moves, with your permission and a readable trail. Records model the world and Transfer coordinates change between people; Karma turns evidence into action.

It is useful without an LLM. People author exact rules visually, use transparent statistics and deterministic algorithms, and plan over their own goals. A model may be attached as an explicit Signal or author an inert candidate; **model access is optional and grants no special authority.** "Always on" means a supervised runtime keeps draining durable work — never always interrupting, always connected, or always authorized to act.

A rule is one sentence long, and the parts below take that sentence apart: what it watches, when it counts as true, and what it does about it.

} r_K7T3ZG08G5EBWRSTF8NBWMTZYV


Rule (@rule: 0, #instinct, #part-of @karma) {
## One Rule in One Sentence

Everything up to here you do by hand. **Karma** is how Lince does things without you. And it is one sentence long:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: Condition"] --> B["And: Threshold"] --> C["Then: Consequence"]
```

In ordinary language:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: tomorrow is cold"] --> B["And: cold enough to matter"] --> C["Then: need to pack a sweater"]
```

And when the world says otherwise, the same rule simply does nothing:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: tomorrow is mild"] --> B["And: not cold enough"] --> C["Then: nothing happens"]
```

## Condition

The condition is an equation, and it can draw on three kinds of input:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["A Frequency"] --> Z["Condition"]
  B["A shell command"] --> Z
  C["A record's quantity"] --> Z
```

Each contributes a number, and the ranges matter more than they look:

- A **Frequency** gives `0` or `1` — it is the clock. It sits on a **Cadence** ("monthly until June", "every 14th"), and stays 0 until the scheduled instant arrives.

- A **command** gives whatever it prints, read as a number.

- A **record's quantity** gives anything at all, from very negative to very positive.

So a condition is arithmetic over live data. Write it abstractly and then substitute what is actually true right now:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Condition: Data > 5"] --> B["Condition: that record's quantity > 5"]
  B --> C["Condition: 2 > 5"]
  C --> D["Condition: false"]
```

_The same rule, resolved one step at a time against the world as it is._

## Threshold

The threshold is the guard between having a number and doing something about it. The usual one is simply "not zero".

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Condition: 0 (false)"] --> B["Threshold: not 0"] --> C["stops here"]
```

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Condition: 2 (a quantity)"] --> B["Threshold: not 0"] --> C["Consequence runs"]
```

_This is why a Frequency returning 0 is enough to keep a whole rule quiet._

#### In detail

**Purpose:** Decide whether the number the Condition produced means *fire*, kept deliberately small so the interesting part stays in the arithmetic.

**How it works:** *only non-zero*, *anything*, or a comparison against a number. That is the whole vocabulary. When it passes, the Condition's value is carried into the Consequence.


## Consequence

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Consequence"] --> B["Change a record's quantity"]
  A --> C["Run a shell command"]
  A --> D["Run SQL"]
```

The first one is the interesting one, because a changed quantity is itself something another rule can be watching. Rules chain without ever referring to each other:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Condition: record reaches a value"] --> B["Consequence: another record changes"]
  B --> C["Condition: that record changed"]
  C --> D["Consequence: a command runs"]
```

_Nobody wired these two rules together. They meet in the data._

#### In detail

**Purpose:** The thing that actually happens, receiving the number the Condition carried.

**How it works:** An ordered, non-empty list — capture an entry, set or add a quantity, assert or retract an ordinary Record assertion, or set an identity assertion. A list rather than one, because `@wip → @done` is one intention and must be one rule.

**Interacts:** A consequence appends an ordinary signed Fact on the target Record, which is a Record moving, which wakes any rule reading it. Anything leaving the Cell goes through §5's grant and §6's intent instead of firing directly.


## Frequency

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["-1 * (Frequency * record quantity)"] --> B["Threshold"] --> C["another record's quantity"]
```

For most of the month the Frequency is `0`, so the whole equation is `0`, so the threshold stops it and nothing happens. On the scheduled day the Frequency becomes `1`, the equation takes the value of the first record, and that amount moves into the second. A monthly bill, with no scheduler and no special "recurring" feature — just a number that is zero most of the time.


# 1 — Rule

**Purpose:** A Rule is the whole of Karma's authoring surface. It is three parts and nothing else, and it is one object so a person never has to learn where the schedule went.

**How it works:** The Condition computes a number from blocks referenced by slug. The Threshold decides whether that number means *fire*. The Consequence happens and receives the number the Condition carried.

```
-1 * freq(@daily) + @apple    →  !=0  →  add-quantity @pear
└──────── Condition ────────┘    Thresh.   └── Consequence ──┘
```

**Interacts:** Reads Record quantities and Frequencies; writes through Actions, which append Facts, which wake other Rules — that chain is deliberate and is how one Rule's consequence feeds another's condition.

With #fruit, a rule keeps catching Records asserted after it was written — you don't revisit the rule. Renaming a concept is free: rules keep working and show the new name at once. #fruta and #Frucht write the same rule — each person reads it in their own name for it, and naming two aliases of one concept twice in one rule is refused as the duplicate it is. Writing a rule with a name that means two concepts is refused there and then, so you say which. (And: reusing a deleted @slug points old rules at the new Record.)
}

Trail (@trail: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #part-of @ontology, #done) { r_Q46Z5FZHZFACPNSNPJP2GEDC1D
Alexandria was a library inside a temple, well maintained and kept, in a city with a port, many travelers where asked to hand their books and manuscripts and receive a copy instead. While there is the common imagination that it was burned, the details are a little conflicting. What remains from the story is the idea of a great body of knowledge, that worked because it was cared for, and the fact that it can suddenly catch fire and be lost. Great care can be put into maintaining and expanding knowledge. It will most likely provide itself useful for those meeting Needs.

In Lince, the Alexandria vibe means sharing knowledge of what things are, how they work, their consequences, and how to implement them. We've come a long way, we should record that, the Trail we made should be known by others. We hope people can learn from us. Catch up on latest wisdom and knowledge. Understand how something works, how to build it, reproduce it, make it better, turn it into their own. Trail is how we call the exporting and importing of Lince data.

It should come in as a package, one can put into the db and start using it in a useful way right away. It should be able to come with Records that describe what things are, how they work, what they need to do to make it work. How can they prove it is real/right. What tasks, recurrent or not, need to be made for it to function, what processes of transfer could/should be made for it to be actualized. The steps for something to be achieved, refined over centuries, by billions of people, with normal Lince workflows of collective improvement. People create useful patterns, import information from other systems, all we need to turn every piece of wisdom and knowledge from the world into something we can hold is a button for exporting part of the data you hold, and a button to import what you want.

This isnt a big feature, it is an abstraction. A small thing to turn data into value, a Trail one can follow, with steps ranging from online personal education, to how to build a school, how to organize the learning of the teachers and how do they rotate the student's learning across several subjects for maximum development. A good Lince will allow for this Trail to be edited, agreed upon, by many people, before it is is unwrapped and it's tasks assigned, and the work done by each envolved.

The first Trail is Instinct, the tutorial of Lince that comes with every copy, as a component with text that can turn into Records, for one to manage the progress of their knowledge of Lince, some may be in -1 quantity, needing to be read and understood, others are 1 quantity, done... There might be various ways to import data into Lince, they may all be called a Trail one can follow, or diverge from. There is a lot of work to build a body of knowledge capable of supporting a society from scratch, that's a prerequisite for Lince to sustain itself, right...? Let's pretend that we do need a highly organized, advanced and functional society for Lince to be actualized, then it is Lince's job to make sure that those that want to build it have the means necessary for doing so, and the knowledge for that, from the chopping of wood, to the chip making must be well documented. That means an open university style of knowledge accumulation, with ways to embed that into a Lince when building it's code or linking it to be accessed when using Lince. Since we are going to be pretentious we need to deliver, we must make the tool a silver bullet for reaching the philosophy, complete.

Now the question remains, how do we integrate with other systems, how do we make sure we can download wikipedia and other bases of knowledge and Lince's own file system for data storage and put all into one place that gets ingested as one or at least interacted in Lince as if it where all the same thing, turning the many different sources of information into actionable tasks and knowledge?

Trails of certain types cannot be maintained by non-technical people of such area probably, one can teach itself, but it should be curated, and updated, as these fields have advancements everyday. A good Lince will be able to remove friction between generating and proving knowledge inside Lince or not, and having them accessible as a Trail, managing data is a Need, we should attend to it.

People might make Trails have checkpoints, knowing certain things perfectly advances you to next level, that can be a general Engineering skill, with specific Trails for different Engineering fields. These checkpoints can be 0 for starting people and 100 for maximum knowledge of the field (perfection).

Trails shouldnt be rigid, because everything is connected, we just dont want to speak about everything when we speak about something because it takes too long, forever and is not that useful to mention to a certain situation. We limit a trail to a subset of knowledge, actions, processes, because it is deemed to be useful to our Needs, our workflows. You may probably always find a way to utilize skills from a field in another, that might be a talend you have developed. But the appearances is that such skills are not that related to the new field you are developing yourself in you will be able to master such field much faster if you dont learn the previous one first, doing prior work might always be useful, but not optimal, and that is the reason that fields, their knowledge and requirements should be separated (at first at least, specialization is always possible after you master the basics). If you want to go into computer science, it might help to know about marble sculpting. But if you want to understand computers, how to calculate hexadecimal from binary is best to study in relation to different types of chisels if you just have one night. With that in mind, we separate the fields.

Here are the subjects wanted to create Trails for, from fields of knowledge to simple daily workflows, fabrication of items...:

- [ ] Basic Economic Transfers - these might really like certain features that have some specific combinations, being useful to make a Castle that is an easy way to enact certain workflows. People can download these Trails for living in cities, as templates on how to use Lince for doing common things like buying seats at an event, cinema, restaurant reservations, or ride sharing, supermarket purchases, all as preset workflows, Trails.
  - [ ] Ride sharing
  - [ ] Grocery Shopping
  - [ ] Reservations and ticket buying in certain places.
  - [ ] Circular tool sharing.

- [ ] Advanced Economic Endeavors:
  - [ ] Ergos - The product of sweat to build and maintain a complex production society.
    - [ ] From mineration to mechanical machines and computers (needs probably to be intertwined with other parts, there should be instructions from using hands to break trees to building wood pickaxes to creating GPUs).
    - [ ] STEM:
      - [ ] Math
      - [ ] Physics
      - [ ] Chemistry
      - [ ] Production Engineering:
        - [ ] Production Planning Programming and Control.
      - [ ] Computer Science:
        - [ ] Data Structures and Algorithms
        - [ ] Eletronics
        - [ ] Programming Languages
        - [ ] Operating Systems
        - [ ] Software Engineering

- [ ] Karma Trails so we can do certain workflows like: daily habits, to bureaucracy one has to do if they live in some country, with a lot of resources to understand how to havigate them, or given some demand, how to keep something supplied for a week, so i can plug my records in it and get to work.
- [ ] Health, can be a big bundle made of optional smaller bundle of Trails, to ingest just one part you want:
  - [ ] Phisio: A 3D representation of your body that can be overlapped with a textbook representation as to how it should function. Useful for tracking together with health professionals the state of your health. From skin evolution to blood test measurement over your age and conditions, to glucose levels, expected range of motion, normal pain points, allergies, ancestors medical history, all imported and managed with correct interface.
  - [ ] Demeter Pathway (like in Lord of Mysteries): how to organize the food you eat, according to many different diets. With steps on how to plant/acquire it (scanning possible vendors around you with Transfer proposals if you want it), treat it, and cook it, separating into several portions, reading data from your measurements to determine the amount of your Needs of certain foods and what you must avoid due to alergies (one shoudl use a Medical Lingua Vocabulary, to read your records of alergies and have the exact term of an alergy the Trail can query to cancel the recommendation and alert you of things you should avoid, avoid eating together, avoid eating at all if it even may contain something you can ingest due to a processing error).
	- [x] Nutrients
	- [ ] Farming
	- [ ] Meal Preparation
	- [ ] Interface for that

  ## Optimizer

  It had also an optimizer for price of the items and to get all the nutrients you needed with least amount of price and restrictions on minimum and maximum amount of grams per item you'd be able to build a set of meals for X days, so it would be a good guide on what groceries to buy. 

  The optimizer minimizes total generic-currency price over food gram variables.
  It uses a two-phase simplex tableau with these constraints:

  - per-food min, max, and forced grams
  - total marmita volume from pot count and pot volume
  - minimum calories from Mifflin-St Jeor estimated expenditure
  - minimum daily protein
  - minimum daily fiber

  If the LP is infeasible, the UI reports the violated class of constraint and
  keeps a generated fallback rather than silently producing a broken plan.

} r_Q46Z5FZHZFACPNSNPJP2GEDC1D

